"ph":6.1,"ph_class":"slightly acid","stones":15,"cells":13225},"planted":{"scope":"own","names":["Baden"],"year":2016,"ha":14666,"grapes":[["Pinot Noir",5257,"R","Pinot Noir"],["Müller-Thurgau",2312,"W","Müller-Thurgau"],["Pinot Gris",1766,"G","Pinot Gris"],["Pinot Blanc",1273,"W","Pinot Blanc"],["Riesling",1029,"W","Riesling"],["Chasselas",1020,"W","Chasselas"],["Pinot Meunier",234,"R","Pinot Meunier"],["Regent",224,"R","Regent"],["Chardonnay",186,"W","Chardonnay"],["Gewürztraminer",138,"W","Gewürztraminer"],["Cabernet Mitos",125,"R","Cabernet Mitos"],["Silvaner",120,"W","Silvaner"],["Other",982,"","other"]],"legal":["main","main","main","main","main","no","no","main","main","main","main","no",""]},"terrain":{"elev":[120,210,430],"slope":4.7,"flat":0.47,"steep":0.16,"zoom":11,"tiles":16,"cells":540731},"area_km2":13193,"rocks":{"families":[["granite",0.33],["sand",0.26],["shale",0.22],["loess",0.15]],"points":9,"scale":"medium","names":{"granite":"Granite","sand":"Sand","shale":"Shale and mudstone","loess":"Loess"}},"large":false,"reading":{"climate":[["Grapes","Seasons this cool ripen Müller-Thurgau, Pinot Gris, Riesling and Gewürztraminer fully without cooking off their freshness."],["Grown here","Müller-Thurgau, Pinot Gris and Riesling sit in their sweet spot, so the wines can show the style they are known for."],["Grown here","Pinot Noir is at its cool limit. In cool years it can stay underripe, with green, sharp flavors."]],"soil":{"rock":"Granite weathers to coarse, acid, free-draining soil that warms quickly and holds heat. Its acidity tends to lower the grapes' acidity, which suits Gamay. Sand is warm and airy. It drains fast but holds little water, and phylloxera does not thrive in it.","top":"The topsoil holds water well and drains at a steady pace, so vines rarely go thirsty or sit in wet ground. Its pH is close to neutral, so vines take up the nutrients they need easily."},"land":[["Height","The high ground runs about 4°F cooler than the low ground. Higher vineyards ripen later and keep more acidity, so one grape can make lighter or richer wine here depending on height."],["Slope","Gentle slopes. Enough tilt to drain cold air and rain away, which lowers frost risk, yet still easy to farm by machine."]]},"winkler":["Ia","Only very early grapes ripen well, mostly hybrids and a few vinifera."],"wset":true,"sources":[{"name":"Wikidata sitelinks and Wikimedia page views","url":"https://www.wikidata.org"},{"name":"Candiago et al. 2022, A geospatial inventory of regulatory information for wine protected designations of origin in Europe, Scientific Data (product specifications as of 2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01513-0"},{"name":"Fick and Hijmans 2017, WorldClim 2.1 monthly averages for 1970 to 2000, 2.5 minute grid (about 4.5 km)","url":"https://www.worldclim.org/data/worldclim21.html"},{"name":"Beck et al. 2023, High-resolution (1 km) Koppen-Geiger maps, Scientific Data (1991 to 2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02549-6"},{"name":"Winkler, Cook, Kliewer and Lider 1974, General Viticulture, University of California Press (heat summation regions)","url":null},{"name":"Wikipedia, Winkler index (what each region suits)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Winkler_index&oldid=1379995815"},{"name":"Jones 2010, Climate, grapes, and wine: structure and suitability in a variable and changing climate, VIII International Terroir Congress (which grapes suit which growing season temperatures)","url":"https://ives-openscience.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Climate_Grape_Wine_Jones.pdf"},{"name":"ISRIC SoilGrids 2.0, 1 km aggregates, 30 to 60 cm depth (CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://www.isric.org/explore/soilgrids"},{"name":"USDA NRCS Soil Survey Manual, Agriculture Handbook 18 (texture classes and pH classes)","url":null},{"name":"Anderson, Nelgen and Puga, Database of Regional, National and Global Winegrape Bearing Areas by Variety, 2000 to 2023, University of Adelaide (CC BY 4.0)","url":"